WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Request logging helps teams understand application behavior across endpoints and services.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:17.960
In ASP.NET Core, request logs can show which route pattern was used, which service handled the request, how long it took, what outcome category occurred, and whether downstream dependencies were involved.

3
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.060
That view is valuable for operations and security response.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:28.740
Correlation IDs, request IDs, trace IDs, and span IDs connect related activity.

5
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:39.720
A single user action may pass through middleware, a controller, a service, a database call, an outbound client, a queue message, and a background worker.

6
00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:45.660
ExampleCorrelationId gives responders a way to follow that path without recording raw payloads everywhere.

7
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:50.500
Request logs should show flow without exposing sensitive content.

8
00:00:50.600 --> 00:01:02.920
Safe context often includes route pattern, HTTP method category, status category, duration, service name, dependency name, and stable non-sensitive identifiers.

9
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:11.980
It usually should not include full URLs, query strings, headers, cookies, request bodies, or response bodies when those values may contain sensitive data.

10
00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:17.620
Distributed systems need consistent correlation across service-to-service calls.

11
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:26.440
Outbound clients, background jobs, message handlers, and scheduled tasks should preserve or create trace context in a predictable way.

12
00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:32.940
Without consistency, each service may have logs that are locally useful but hard to combine during an incident.

13
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:36.500
Correlation also supports audit review and troubleshooting.

14
00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:46.680
It lets teams connect a user report to protected logs, connect a deployment event to later failures, and connect a security event to the downstream actions that followed.

15
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.740
Good correlation reduces the urge to log sensitive payloads just to understand sequence.

16
00:01:52.020 --> 00:02:00.580
The same conventions should apply across ASP.NET Core middleware, controllers, minimal APIs, background jobs, and outbound clients.

17
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:07.480
When each layer uses different context names or loses the request identity, investigations become slower and less reliable.
